## 6. Trade-Offs & Design Decisions

| Decision | What We Chose | Alternative Considered | Trade-Off & Justification |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Queue Broker** | **Redis + BullMQ** | RabbitMQ / Apache Kafka / AWS SQS | BullMQ provides sub-millisecond job distribution, native Redis data structures, job deduplication, and low operational overhead. Kafka would be heavy overkill for a submission queue; RabbitMQ adds an additional service dependency. |
| **Result Retrieval** | **Short Polling** (`GET /api/submissions/:id`) | WebSockets / Server-Sent Events (SSE) | Polling is stateless, survives reverse-proxy reconnects, requires zero persistent TCP socket state on the API servers, and is straightforward to scale behind load balancers. WebSockets add connection state complexity. |
| **Worker Boundary** | **Separate OS Process** (`src/worker.ts`) | Shared in-process threads / cluster module | A separate process guarantees fault isolation and allows worker auto-scaling independently from the API server. |
| **Deduplication Key** | **Database submission UUID as BullMQ `jobId`** | Auto-generated BullMQ job UUID | Matching `jobId` to `submissionId` guarantees idempotency in Redis. If a network blip duplicates an enqueue call, Redis silently deduplicates the job. |

